  :adr/body
  "## Context

`kotoba-lang/loop-innen` grows a sourced dependency record over human history.
Its corpus and ledger stop on 2026-08-12. Ten days are missing.

`scripts/tick.cljs` said, in its own docstring, that the loop was `registered
with tamaki and run by launchd`. Measured 2026-08-22, **neither half was true**:
`tamaki` listed loop-innen only as an example line in its README, and no launchd
job for it existed on the operator machine while a dozen sibling residencies
(`com.gftd.observatory-run`, `com.gftd.fleet-ci-tip-tick`, and others) were
loaded.

The docstring was almost certainly accurate when written. Nothing checked it, so
it went on asserting a residency that had ceased to exist, and it read as a
description of the present.

## Decision

### D1 — the residency is registered as a file, not as a sentence

`scripts/com.kotoba.innen-tick.plist` (`com.kotoba.innen-tick`), launchd at
02:47 / 08:47 / 14:47 / 20:47, running the tick through `tamaki exec` so each
tick is one `AgentRun` with a real exit code while
`ledger/loop-innen-ledger.edn` keeps the per-cycle detail.

launchd rather than a new scheduler, for the reason the sibling observatory
plist gives: this workspace already put its periodic work there, and a second
scheduler splits the answer to `what is running here` in two.

The plist carries the same warning the observatory plist does — two consecutive
hyphens are illegal inside an XML comment, and a malformed plist does not fail
loudly; launchd simply declines to load it, which is the least visible way for a
residency to be absent.

### D2 — verify a residency by running it, not by installing it

Three real defects were found, each only by ACTUALLY RUNNING the job. None was
visible to the unit suite, which stayed green throughout.

**(a) A bare double quote inside a docstring stopped every entry point.**
Introduced while correcting the very sentence D1 is about. The docstring ended
at the quote, the next word was read as a symbol, and every invocation died with
`registered is not ISeqable`. The suite exercises `src/` and never opens a
script, so it reported green while nothing could run.

Counting quotes would NOT have caught it: the broken line carried two of them,
parity was preserved, and the file held 222 unescaped quotes — an even number, a
file that looks balanced. This is the trap CLAUDE.md already records for heredoc
EDN, reappearing in a Clojure docstring.

**(b) Publication was structurally impossible in a west checkout.** The tick ran
`git push origin main`. west names the remote after the manifest entry
(`kotoba-lang`) and leaves the tree on a DETACHED HEAD, so both halves were
false: no such remote, no local ref named `main`. The last successful tick is
immediately before the commit named `rescue/west-detached-20260812`. Publication
has been impossible since that rescue while every ingest kept reporting success.

**(c) The git helper discarded stderr.** It read stdout and the exit code and
threw the error text away, so the residency printed `FAIL publish (exit 1)` for
a failure git had explained in full. This is why (b) lasted ten days: the cause
was one line away and nothing could show it. Third of the five questions in
ADR-2608136000.

### D3 — the gate for (a) is a LOAD, not a lint

Each entry point accepts `--parse-only`, read after the whole file has been read
and compiled; reaching it proves the file is well formed, and the script exits
before any work or network I/O. `loop-innen.scripts-load-test` spawns all four.

A lint would have to model the reader. A load IS the reader.

### D4 — publish moved into `src/` so it could be tested at all

`loop-innen.publish` resolves the remote (preferring `origin` when it exists, so
ordinary clones are unaffected) and the remote's default branch instead of
assuming them, and pushes `HEAD:<branch>` so a detached checkout can publish.
The push is not forced and the fast-forward is `--ff-only`: a divergence is
git's refusal, not something this code decides. `git` returns `{:exit :out :err}`
always, and a failed step carries `:failed-at` and the text.

### D5 — one parser, because there were four

Each of four scripts carried its own `partition-all 2` argument parser and all
four had the same defect: a boolean flag consumed the NEXT FLAG'S NAME as its
value. `--no-push --root /p` parsed as `{:no-push \"--root\"}` and dropped the
path; `--root` then fell back to `../../..`, which from
`orgs/kotoba-lang/loop-innen` IS the superproject. So production was correct BY
POSITION, argument order silently decided what was ingested, and nothing went
red. Invoked the other way round, the workspace ingest walked a directory that
does not exist and reported `ok ingest:workspace (exit 0) — 0 nodes / 0 edges`:
a successful ingest of an empty world.

`loop-innen.cli` is now the only parser. Every valued option goes through
`string-opt`, so a bare `--root` cannot hand `true` to a filesystem path.

### D6 — regression tests must be able to fail on the code they replace

Every repair here is tested against the OLD behaviour as well as the new one:
`the-old-parser-is-shown-to-be-wrong` reproduces the shipped parser and asserts
it differs; `the-old-command-fails-on-the-same-fixture` runs the shipped
`git push origin main` on the west-shaped fixture and asserts it fails;
reintroducing the exact docstring reds `every-entry-point-loads`.

A regression test that cannot fail on the code it replaces proves nothing.

## Consequences

- The record grows again without a human. Verified end to end under launchd:
  `LastExitStatus 0`, all four steps ok, commit `6275b85` pushed by the job
  itself, ledger at `:event/seq 33`.
- A resident loop that commits four times a day means the west pin for this
  repository will lag by design between advances. That is normal for a data
  repository and is not drift to chase.
- The failed kickstart runs left an automated commit in the SHARED west checkout
  (`:event/seq 32`, 459 nodes / 2417 edges — a real measurement). It was
  preserved through a rescue branch and a server-side merge, not discarded and
  not rebased.
- The lesson generalises past this repository: **a claim about the world that
  nothing checks decays into a claim about the past.** Docstrings that assert
  deployment state, READMEs that name a schedule, and manifests that describe a
  gate are all subject to it. The remedy is not more careful prose; it is to
  make the claim executable, or to date it and expect it to rot.

## Evidence

Measured 2026-08-22 at `kotoba-lang/loop-innen` 6275b85:

- suite 26 tests / 102 assertions, 0 failures (was 10 / 63)
- publish tested against real temporary git repositories arranged in the failing
  configuration: a bare remote, a checkout wired under a NON-origin remote name,
  left on a detached HEAD. Covers publishing from it, no-remote refused with a
  reason, a non-fast-forward stopping at `ff`, and the helper returning error
  text.
- one launchd run end to end: ingest:wikidata 142 nodes / 125 edges,
  ingest:workspace 317 / 185, cycle ok, publish ok, record 459 nodes /
  2417 edges. Critical nodes surfaced include `jurisdiction-us-de`
  (43 dependents), `world-war-i` (23), `paris-peace-conference` (22) and
  `molotov-ribbentrop-pact` (17)."}]
